<p>(This started as a reply above, but jumping down to a new chain so it's not so horizontally compressed.)</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I suppose something I could do is to eliminate all narrative/reasoning portions from this concept and only put down what he is and what he is capable of doing.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That would be the opposite of progress here.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>There isn't really a section in the template for SCP Abilities.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Correct. There is a reason for this. SCPs are not merely strange things with lists of abilities, they are stories.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;my&nbsp;suggestion&nbsp;for&nbsp;where&nbsp;to&nbsp;go&nbsp;from&nbsp;here</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Chisel&nbsp;until&nbsp;the&nbsp;parts&nbsp;that&nbsp;don't&nbsp;look&nbsp;like&nbsp;a&nbsp;statue&nbsp;are&nbsp;gone.</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>First things first, pitfall wise, you're 100% looking at walking a tightrope over Edgelord Canyon here. This guy could easily tip over into the abyss of edgy 90's comic book characters that have names spelled with statistically disproportionate quantities of &quot;x&quot;s and &quot;y&quot;s&#8230; but if you can make this guy a rounded, grounded character it could actually work.</p>
<p>I can see a glimmer of raw diamond here, but the gem metaphor applies heavily: the jeweler's task is all about precisely cutting away the extraneous material to leave only the most interesting parts.<br />
Reading through what you've said so far, I spot the makings of a viable elevator pitch that makes my ears perk up-</p>
<ul>
<li><em>&quot;He doesn't see someone's destiny. He sees their &quot;supposed&quot; destiny, or what it is that they were meant to do in life, the thing that they are driven towards or driven to do.&quot;</em></li>
<li><em>&quot;wears an assortment of anomalous artifacts that are melded with his body.&quot;</em></li>
<li>Jack is a part of Twist's mind</li>
<li>the general idea of him pursuing a greater, possibly even benevolent, goal in this hunt he's on</li>
</ul>
<p>The first one is all the anomaly you need to set you up with an interesting character: A guy with a simple but potent ability chasing pieces of a domino chain that could lead to a cataclysmic end scenario that isn't necessarily going to happen.</p>
<p>Not necessarily suggesting any direct/overt connection, but I immediately jump to <a href="/scp-4239">SCP-4239</a>- (one of the GAW's Misters Against Weed, Mr. Destiny). His anomaly enables people to find their destiny in this exact sense- not a predetermined fate, but rather their driving purpose and their potential for doing the right thing at the right time to set further things in motion. Mostly just pointing it out as an interesting opposite thematically, but there's also big-picture potential for them to at least have intersecting interests at some point.</p>
<p>But back to the current idea: to riff off of what Cybersquid said, the interest here for me lives in that ambiguous intersection and contrast of &quot;<em>Twist is a ruthless murderer chasing shadows that might only exist in his own head</em>&quot; and &quot;<em>Twist is an anti-hero who will stop at nothing to prevent the destruction of the world</em>&quot;. He's not an all-knowing unstoppable monster&#8230; just a man who sees a little more than others, with an obsession that may or may not be justified, and a few tricks up his sleeve.</p>
<p>Regarding the grab bag of grafted-on anomalies, the bootleg cyborg thing is a vibe I'm personally a fan of in general, but in this case <em>keep it grounded</em>. A laundry list of abilities isn't going to interest the audience, and pulling out a new gadget every two minutes to do exactly whatever he wants is cartoon supervillain territory. Use simple things in a clever way, though and you paint a picture of a resourceful and competent threat building on past successes.</p>
<p>You've got a great way to organically tie this part of his concept back into the rest, making it even more interesting and playing up that &quot;magnificent bastard&quot; aspect at the same time: <em>What if he can see the potential in</em> things, <em>too?</em> You could get a ton of milage out of having most of his collection being the sort of thing you'd see on the Anomalous Items List, combined in clever ways because he can see their potential. This can complicate his moral standing in odd ways, too- say, he knows that item A vastly increases his ability to stop the cataclysm, but item A is a piece of paratech in the head of a particular unimportant CotBG member. It's nothing special, but it has to be <em>that</em> one.</p>
<p>More flashy components could also be used to hint at past exploits, or even as the basis for tales in themselves. One suggestion I'd throw out is that <a href="/scp-748">SCP-748</a>-5 instances (I won't spoil the non-essential details) have integrated phasing tech- the catch being that their tech self-destructs on termination so the unit would have to be <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">alive</span> functional during the transplant process, and that it's probably not going to be the most stable item in his kit even if both he and the device survive the process.</p>
<p>Lastly, while the &quot;tightrope over Edgelord Canyon&quot; warning remains the big flashing red danger sign here, I'd strongly recommend embracing the edginess a <em>little</em> bit if you go the tale series route- like there's some great potential for zombie-stomping action-horror here if you can keep it on the rails in the process.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

