<blockquote>
<p>He's not an all-knowing unstoppable monster… just a man who sees a little more than others, with an obsession that may or may not be justified, and a few tricks up his sleeve.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is a part of the vibe that I am trying to create. In a one on one scenario, Twist can be very intimidating. However, put a couple more people in the room and he is far less deadly and easier to contain due to the nature of how his anomalous traits work, which I planned to explain in the article.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>What if he can see the potential in things, too?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The short answer would be yes. So long as the object is within his line of sight, he can view this.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>A laundry list of abilities isn't going to interest the audience</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I completely agree. Anything he has in his arsenal I already have inventoried. It's not going to be a batman utility belt scenario. I have some stories in mind for this character within the SCP universe with a few of them focusing on how he got some of his artifacts. However, one of the critiques that I get here is that the one ability I do mention (instantly knowing things about stuff he sees) is <strong>boring/uninteresting or broken</strong>. He does have other capabilities that I haven't mentioned here, but I haven't mentioned them because they aren't crucial to the story or to what the story is supposed to be focusing on, which is supposed to be about the SCP foundation trying to get information out of Twist and figuring out the rules to obtaining this information. So, to placate the critique of &quot;uninteresting anomalous ability&quot;, I mentioned a few other items/powers. In response to your review in particular though, I have since removed them.</p>
</div>
