<p>{manifests again in response to PM}</p>
<p>Okay, this is going to be a bit of a back-and forth dialogue if you really want to forge this idea into shape, but since you seem serious about it let's dig in:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;sculpture&nbsp;is&nbsp;still&nbsp;under&nbsp;the&nbsp;rock</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;more&nbsp;chiseling</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>one of the critiques that I get here is that the one ability I do mention (instantly knowing things about stuff he sees) is boring/uninteresting or broken.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>To put it simply, they're right. That can be fixed, though.</p>
<p>Firstly, heed Quadraginta's critique in particular- the traits they're pointing to are part of that Edgelord Canyon I mentioned you're trying to tightrope across. You might never win them over, but they're pointing out a lot of the pitfalls you need to avoid. (De-emphasizing the middle name to an initial is a good first move, for instance- was going to mention that being a little on-the-nose in the previous critique myself and forgot.)</p>
<p>Two things in your reply above sort of&#8230; bracket both what I've actually been finding interesting about this guy and what you'll want to avoid. I think it might be backwards from what you're thinking, though:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It's not going to be a batman utility belt scenario.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is actually the less important of the two things, but Batman and his utility belt are a really good example to follow for this guy's collection of anomalies.</p>
<p>We're never told explicitly what's in the utility belt, and with Batman's resources and skills the options are pretty much limited only by what can plausibly fit in the belt. What good Batman writers figure out, though, is that the utility belt is boring when Batman has a gadget that does anything he needs to do for him. Used at its best, the belt always has what <em>Batman</em> needs to do anything <em>in the most interesting way possible</em>.</p>
<p>Same thing here: even you as the writer don't need to know every item Twist has. He can have whatever you want him to have- so long as it's in the name of doing something in the most interesting way possible. That's why I suggested emphasizing that most of his collection are innocuous and/or useless seeming items- they're things that only he would see the potential in. It's boring if he has a gizmo that lets him fly. It's interesting if he combines three minor anomalies in an way that causes him to be hurled through the air havok-physics-glitch style.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I haven't mentioned them because they aren't crucial to the story or to what the story is supposed to be focusing on, which is supposed to be about the SCP foundation trying to get information out of Twist and figuring out the rules to obtaining this information.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Again, the power isn't the interesting part, and the whole &quot;the Foundation NEEDS the information&quot; thing is a bit of a site cliche with oracles. (They might like to have said information, but there's loads of other POIs they'd love to put under a microscope, too.) I'd actually even suggest toning it down further, so that he doesn't have instant absolute insight into everything- if he just gets incomplete flashes and glimpses of insight its both a great narrative tool for foreshadowing and &quot;how we got here&quot; storytelling, and a much less &quot;game-breaking&quot; power.</p>
<p>Vincent Anderson of <a href="/anderson-robotics-hub">Anderson Robotics</a> might be a good bit of site lore to look at regarding one way you could approach telling this guy's story. He's a literal cyborg sorcerer with all the makings of a stereotypical edgelord OC by the end of his arc- but his messy, complicated story about a man trying to find an offramp on that road paved with good intentions makes him one of the most interesting characters on the site. In Twist, you've got a similar chance to show us a really simple, fairly weak precognitive/clairvoyant type power leading a guy down the road to become one of the SCPverse's many boogymen.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

