<p><em>Responding to PM Request.</em></p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+Let's&nbsp;do&nbsp;it!</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-Let's&nbsp;do&nbsp;it!</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I'm going to keep this relatively simple, short and concise here. He feels 100% like a generic dark, mysterious, edgy anime character with nothing interesting going on at all whatsoever.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Twist is unable to answer specific questions. When he tries to, an anomalous event occurs that blurs his face and distorts his voice to be unrecognizable and indecipherable.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That's way too convenient and forced for its own good.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Twist can instantly know the purpose, function, and method of elimination for any target he sees. The SCP Foundation NEEDS this intel.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This isn't as physically combatively overpowered as Abel, but by sheer function, this already renders him far, <em>far</em> too overpowered and X-Men-ish for his own good.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Anomalous Properties</strong></p>
</blockquote>
<p>I think you mean superpowers, because that's essentially what this guy has here.</p>
<p>And last but not least, and this is admittedly an extremely shallow nitpick: I don't like his name. James DEVILian (Devil?) Twist. The words 'Devil' and 'Twist' are in his name. It really doesn't get any more anime edgey than that.</p>
<p>I apologise if this came off as extremely harsh, but I'm not feeling this at all in the slightest.</p>
<p>I'm also not really a fan of edgy anime characters, so I was kinda&#8230;completely the wrong person to crit this.</p>
<p>I also completely forgot to cover the plot here, basically because it doesn't have one.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The core plot of this article is the SCP foundation's various attempts to uncover secret information that Twist holds.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This explains literally nothing. He has secrets. What are those secrets? You can make them secretive if you manage to get to drafting this, but for the idea pitch, please give us more information on what those secrets are.</p>
<p>Yeah, I'm sorry if this disappoints, but I have nothing to offer here in terms of suggestions or recommendations or tips. I can only state what I disliked about this piece, and it was essentially all of it. I deeply apologise for that.</p>
<p>I do hope you stay and pursue a work on this site, though, as the Greenlight process can be brutal. Don't be discouraged. The trip to one's first SCP is worth the struggle.</p>
<p>Anywho, I bid you a good day. 👍</p>
<p><strong>P.S.</strong> I didn't even acknowledge the crosslink idea. Here's the most generally agreed upon consensus for crosslinking on one's first SCP: don't. Many would view it as trying to ride off the success of more popular articles. You'll need to be a lot more familiar with how things go here first before crosslinking will become a good idea. A first SCP has to be able to stand on its own.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

